{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>See now that I, I.</b> The word אני indicates an existent, like אתה, and likewise the word הוא indicates an existent also, that is, He exists forever; and behold, both of them are names, in the manner of what is said (Sukkah, chapter 4): אני והו הושיעה נא. And I have already explained to you in the section ויהי בשלח (Exodus 15:2), in the verse זה אלי ואנוהו, that the operation of these names is for love and to gather the scattered. And Moses wished to seal this song with the promise of the future redemption in the ingathering of the scattered of Israel, and therefore he says ראו עתה כי אני אנה הוא — that is, I am HaShem, known through the ingathering of the scattered. And afterward he said אני אמית ואחיה — a allusion to the resurrection of the dead, which is adjacent to the redemption. And afterward he said חי אנכי לעולם, alluding to the World to Come that comes after the resurrection, for the children of the resurrection will live for the World to Come and never die, as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: the dead whom the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to revive will never again return to their dust, but will be transported from the world of resurrection to the World to Come, in body and in soul. And concerning the World to Come that comes after the resurrection, our Rabbis, of blessed memory, say: it is always prepared for the life of the World to Come, and it is the abundance of good stored away, the ultimate reward and delight for the house of Israel; and concerning it the prophet, peace be upon him, said (Isaiah 64:3): ״עין לא ראתה אלהים זולתך״, and he said thus because of the abundance of its concealment. And from this is written לעלם defective, without a ו׳, in the verse (Exodus 3:15) ״זה שמי לעלם״, which our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: לעלם is written (i.e., defective, related to לעלם — to conceal). And since up to here the words of Moses, who rebukes Israel and makes known to them the future things, now Scripture comes to speak in the language of the Holy One, blessed be He, and to announce to them Himself, with the promise of redemption in the ingathering of the scattered of Israel, and in the matter of the resurrection of the dead, and in the World to Come — and then He will cause the nations to sing."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The Hebrew text as supplied contains 'אנה הוא' which appears to be a typo for 'אני הוא'; translated faithfully as it appears. The citation '(סוכה פרק ד)' refers to m. Sukkah 4:5. Verse citation for Isaiah follows the printed text (Isaiah 64:3 in Hebrew verse numbering = 64:4 in some English numbering)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:53:35.853805623Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}